item.description:
Choosing the Best Database? &ndash;
Whether you are an experienced software engineer or a student doing a university project, at some point we will need to choose a DB for our project.
Choosing the right kind of database always requires some consideration.
&ndash;
First of all, databases will not impact our functional requirements. &ndash;
Whichever database we use can still achieve our functional requirements somehow, but at the cost of huge performance degradation.

Message queues &hellip;📨 📨
The role of message queuing in a micro-service architecture.
&ndash;
When is the right time to use a message queue and why is a database rarely the best tool for a queue-based problem? A blog on backend engineering&hellip;
Imagine that we have a web application for customers to upload a text document, which then converts it into a PDF, and then emails it back to the customer.

&ndash;
How often do you shard your database? Do you prematurely shard? Do you actually need that for your database?🤔 &ndash;
Analogy: Your application is growing. It has more active users, more features, and generates more data every day.
Your database is now becoming a bottleneck for the rest of your application. Database sharding could be the solution to your problems.
But Wait&hellip; Q) What is Database Sharding? &ndash;
&ndash;

Got an old Raspberry Pi lying around? Hate seeing ads while browsing the web? Pi-hole is an open-source software project that blocks ads for all devices on your home network by routing all advertising servers into nowhere. What’s best is it takes just a few minutes to set up.
&ndash;
&ndash;
What is a Pi-hole? Network-wide ad blocking via your own Linux hardware. The Pi-hole® is a DNS sinkhole that protects your devices from unwanted content, without installing any client-side software.
